# What making the Stone Drawer work on both lanes changes on purpose, and why.
#
#   gate_diff.py --expect tools/porcelain_gate/expect/mobile-gameframe-both-lanes.expect <pairs...>
#
# Two things change and they are separable. One is a new ROLE, which every
# OldSchool lane sees and nothing else about them moves. The other is the 601
# chat block, which is the whole of stone601's difference and is not visible on
# any other lane. dat1_254 -- the lane this round is mostly about -- is byte
# identical, because a 2004 profile declares neither the role nor interface
# 162 and the frame under test there is the LANE's, not this plugin's: the
# capture runs `auto`, so the fix that makes the Stone Drawer come up when it
# IS chosen cannot show in it. The cs1live screenshot is where that half is
# judged, and it is judged by looking at it.
#
# Every line here is a claim a reader is meant to be able to disagree with. If
# you think one of them is wrong, the argument is with the reason, not with the
# tool.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. A new role, on every lane whose cache mounts interface 162.
#
# `[role:chat_controls]` names the chat bar's LAYER. [role:chat_bar] names the
# bar's PICTURE and says at length why it must stay the picture: naming the
# layer there once made a "complete replacement" take the whole subtree and
# throw four filters away. This is a second role for a second question --
# geometry -- and nothing that re-skins a chat reaches it. It exists because
# the four OldSchool toplevels disagree about which end of the block the bar
# goes on and a frame that wants the same chat on both has to move it.
#
# It fires seven times: six CS2 lanes plus the one it is for. A role that
# resolves is a ROLE_WIDGET line, and a line that was not there before reads to
# the comparator as a role that moved -- which is the only kind it has for
# something that arrives.
role-move chat_controls = the revconfig adds this role; it resolves on every lane that mounts interface 162, and on six of them that is the only difference there is

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The 601 chat block, turned the right way up.
#
# The mobile toplevel pins `controls` to the TOP of the pack with the message
# lines underneath. The Stone Drawer's chat is built the other way round -- the
# torn sheet, the stone strip and the two switches all assume the bar is the row
# along the bottom -- so the filters stood on the parchment's torn top edge with
# nothing under the last message line.
#
# It is TWO moves and no re-parenting. The pack goes up by the 30-row band the
# bar takes off its top, so the bottom-anchored message area lands on the
# block's first row (chat_backing 58,384 -> 58,354); the bar goes to the
# block's last rows (chat_controls 58,356 -> 58,471). The assembly occupies
# exactly the rows it did -- 354 through 499 -- which is why the tap blocker,
# the two switches and the strip do not appear anywhere below.
#
# 3,228 capture lines on each side, and they are the same 3,228: 3,224 of the
# pack's own components, its mount, and the three owned controls named under
# section 3. The count is large because the pack carries a text node per
# message row and the whole of it is one block.
only-before BOUNDS com=0x00a2 = interface 162 with its filter bar at the top of the block, which is where toplevel 601 puts it
only-after  BOUNDS com=0x00a2 = the same components with the bar at the bottom: the message area 30 rows up, the bar 115 rows down, the block on the same rows it was
only-before BOUNDS com=0x02590031 = the toplevel's own chat mount, which is the block's parent and moves with it
only-after  BOUNDS com=0x02590031 = the same mount, up by the band the bar no longer takes off the top

# The three of this frame's own pieces that follow the block. An owned control
# carries no component id in a capture -- it is 0xffffffff -- so this is the
# BOUNDS half of the three `owned-move` rows below, said about the same three
# controls. The pair pins itself: if a fourth owned box moved on this lane, the
# `owned-move` list would not cover it and the lane would still fail.
only-before BOUNDS com=0xffffffff = the bar rock, the parchment and the screenshot button at the boxes they had while the bar was on top
only-after  BOUNDS com=0xffffffff = the same three, each following the surface it is pinned to

# The same difference said about the roles rather than the components.
role-move chat_backing = the message area, now the block's first row instead of its last 115
role-move chat_bar = the bar's picture, which follows the layer it is drawn in
role-move chat_input = the input line, which is the message area's last row and went up with it
role-move chat_plate_0 = a filter plate, hidden by this frame and still moved by the bar it hangs on
role-move chat_plate_1 = the same, Game
role-move chat_plate_2 = the same, Public
role-move chat_plate_3 = the same, Private
role-move chat_plate_4 = the same, Channel
role-move chat_plate_5 = the same, Clan
role-move chat_plate_6 = the same, Trade
role-move chat_plate_7 = the Report plate, which this toplevel keeps hidden and which moved with the bar anyway
role-move public_chat_button = the Public filter's own layer, a child of the bar
role-move report_button = the Report button's layer, a child of the bar
role-move action_frame_chat_button_report_activate = the report button again, under its action role
role-move frame_chat = the frame slot the block is bound to, which is the pack root this frame moved

owned-move bar-rock = the 2004 band with a hollow per filter, drawn at the bar's box: it moved because the bar did
owned-move pack-sheet = the torn parchment, positioned from the backing's box: it moved because the message area did
owned-move camera_report = the screenshot plugin's button, which places itself against the report button this frame moved
